PlaceBuffer
PlaceBuffer
Use the object PlaceBuffer to process parts on a number of buffer places, which are arranged in a row, one
behind the other. It is not part of the built-in objects that the Toolbox provides by default.
Image
Description
The MUs, which the PlaceBuffer processes, have to advance from place to place and can only leave the
PlaceBuffer after they passed the last place. This way, you can call and access each and every place
individually.
PlaceBuffer
11-2042 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
We do not show the PlaceBuffer in new models by default. You have to add it yourself to your model.
• If Sequentially Indexing is cleared, you can only set the Processing Time for the PlaceBuffer as a
whole, not for the individual places. The Processing Time is equally distributed across all places. For a
Processing Time of one minute and a Capacity of three places each place has a processing time of 20
seconds.
A part can move directly to an idle place in the PlaceBuffer if Sequentially Indexing is cleared. After the
part has passed this and all succeeding places, it can exit the PlaceBuffer. If you create a part on an idle
place, it does not have to pass this place, but may move on to the next place or to the next object.
• If Sequentially Indexing is checked, the Processing Time for moving the part on to the next place is
used up. For a capacity of four the threefold processing time passes at least before the part can leave
the PlaceBuffer, as three move processes have to take place. The time can be extended through waiting
times.
The PlaceBuffer moves the part cyclically on to its successors. In case the first part cannot leave the
PlaceBuffer, you can set how it moves on:
• To make the MUs accumulate on the PlaceBuffer, select the check box Accumulating. This enables the
MUs to move front to end to each other, when the exit of the PlaceBuffer is Blocked.
• To make the MUs retain their distance to each other, i.e., make all succeeding MUs stop moving when
the preceding MU cannot exit the PlaceBuffer, clear Accumulating.
The default graphic of the PlaceBuffer is a table with a size of 2 by 1 by 1 meter on whose surface up to
four parts are moved on in the X-Direction. When scaling the PlaceBuffer, typically only in the X-Direction,
the pre-defined animation area is scaled as well so that you only have to adjust the Capacity.
We offer two graphics for the PlaceBuffer:
• The graphic Table2x1x1modular.jt in the folder jt-graphics which you can import into any object.
• The alternative graphic PlacebufferModular.s3d in the folder s3d-graphics\BuffersAndSorters, allowing
you to adapt the size of the table to your needs by changing the individually transformable graphics
parts without changing the thickness of the elements of the table.
You can also set the Animation Area on which the PlaceBuffer animates the parts.
Note the following:
1. If the Animation Area is active, the PlaceBuffer uses it. In this case the index of the MU is the index of
the area in the X-Direction. The capacity in the Y-Direction is always 1.
2. If a MU animation path named Default with more than one anchor points exists, the index of the
MU is converted into a relative position. The MUs are then equally distributed on this line.
PlaceBuffer
Plant Simulation Help 11-2043
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

3. In all other cases the PlaceBuffer uses the first path for the first MU, the second path for the second
MU, etc. If no suitable path exists, but a path named Default exists, that consists of a single point
only, the PlaceBuffer uses that path.
If you want to model a buffer with a large capacity that has to be fast, we recommend to use the object
Buffer 
 .
To show a tooltip with information about the PlaceBuffer, hover with the mouse over it.
To change the length of the graphic and the anchor points of the PlaceBuffer, click Show Manipulators
[button] 
  on the Edit ribbon tab or press M on the keyboard.
Add the Object to the Simulation Model
To add the object PlaceBuffer to your simulation model, click 
  Manage Class Library > Basic Objects >
MaterialFlow > PlaceBuffer on the Home ribbon tab.
